{"id": "trm:ekayana", "term": "ekāyana", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10 the path (magga) of the four satipaṭṭhānas is called ekāyana, for the purification of beings, overcoming sorrow and lamentation, ending pain and displeasure, reaching the right method and realising nibbāna. The sutta does not gloss the word; its rendering is disputed.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:2", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:47"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}, "literal": "one-going"}
{"id": "trm:satipatthana", "term": "satipaṭṭhāna", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "Four contemplations, of body, feelings, mind and dhammas, each practised ardent, clearly comprehending and mindful, having put away covetousness and displeasure for the world; the path of purification and realisation of nibbāna (MN 10).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:2", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:3", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:46"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:atapi", "term": "ātāpī", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "One of the three qualities (with sampajāna, satimā) with which the monk abides contemplating in each foundation (MN 10). Not glossed in the sutta.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:3"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:sampajanna", "term": "sampajañña", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10 the monk 'acts with clear comprehension (sampajānakārī)' in going and returning, looking, bending, eating, excreting, sleeping, speaking and keeping silent; it is also one of the qualities of every satipaṭṭhāna practice.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:3", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:8"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:sati", "term": "sati", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10 mindfulness is established in front (parimukhaṁ satiṁ upaṭṭhapetvā) for the breath practice, and 'mindfulness that there is a body (…feelings, mind, dhammas)' is established just for knowledge and recollection (ñāṇamatta, paṭissatimatta).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:4", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:5"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:abhijjhadomanassa", "term": "abhijjhādomanassa", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "What the monk puts away with regard to the world while abiding in each of the four contemplations (MN 10).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:3"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:kayanupassana", "term": "kāyānupassanā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "Contemplation of the body in the body in fourteen sections in MN 10: breath, postures, clear comprehension, repulsiveness, elements and nine charnel-ground contemplations.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:4", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:31"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:vedananupassana", "term": "vedanānupassanā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "Knowing pleasant, painful and neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling, each as worldly (sāmisa) or unworldly (nirāmisa) (MN 10:32).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:32"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:cittanupassana", "term": "cittānupassanā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "Knowing the mind as with or without lust, hatred, delusion; contracted or scattered; exalted or unexalted; surpassable or unsurpassable; concentrated or unconcentrated; liberated or unliberated (MN 10:34).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:34"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:dhammanupassana", "term": "dhammānupassanā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "Contemplation of dhammas in five sections in MN 10: the five hindrances, five aggregates of clinging, six sense bases, seven awakening factors and four noble truths.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:38", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:40", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:42", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:44"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:samisa", "term": "sāmisa", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10:32 feelings are known as worldly (sāmisa) or unworldly (nirāmisa); not glossed in the sutta.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:32"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}, "literal": "with flesh / with bait"}
{"id": "trm:niramisa", "term": "nirāmisa", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The counterpart of sāmisa in MN 10:32; also qualifies rapture (pīti nirāmisā) in MN 118:33.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:32"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}, "literal": "without flesh / without bait"}
{"id": "trm:nivarana", "term": "nīvaraṇa", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The five hindrances named in MN 10:36 as the first field of the contemplation of dhammas: sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, doubt.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:kamacchanda", "term": "kāmacchanda", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The first of the five hindrances in MN 10:36; known as present or absent, and how unarisen arises, arisen is abandoned, abandoned does not arise again.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:byapada", "term": "byāpāda", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The second of the five hindrances (MN 10:36).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:thina-middha", "term": "thinamiddha", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The third of the five hindrances (MN 10:36).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:uddhacca-kukkucca", "term": "uddhaccakukkucca", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The fourth of the five hindrances (MN 10:36).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:vicikiccha", "term": "vicikicchā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The fifth of the five hindrances (MN 10:36).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:36"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:upadanakkhandha", "term": "upādānakkhandha", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The five: form, feeling, perception, formations, consciousness; the monk knows each with its arising and passing away (MN 10:38).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:38"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:ayatana", "term": "āyatana", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The six internal and external bases (eye and forms, ear and sounds, nose and smells, tongue and tastes, body and tangibles, mind and dhammas), with the fetter that arises dependent on each pair (MN 10:40).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:40"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:samyojana", "term": "saṁyojana", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10:40 the fetter that arises dependent on a sense base and its object; known as present, how unarisen arises, how arisen is abandoned, how abandoned does not arise again.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:40"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:bojjhanga", "term": "bojjhaṅga", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The seven: mindfulness, investigation of dhammas, energy, rapture, tranquillity, concentration, equanimity; known as present or absent, how the unarisen arises, how the arisen comes to fulfilment by development (MN 10:42).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:42"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:ariya-sacca", "term": "ariyasacca", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The four known 'as it really is': this is suffering; this is the origin of suffering; this is the cessation of suffering; this is the way leading to the cessation of suffering (MN 10:44).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:44"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:sivathika", "term": "sivathikā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The place where the monk imagines seeing a discarded body, in nine stages of decay, and compares his own body with it (MN 10:14-30).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:14", "tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:30"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:upadisesa", "term": "upādisesa", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "In MN 10:46 the condition under which non-return (anāgāmitā) is the second of the two expected fruits.", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:46"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:anagamita", "term": "anāgāmitā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The second of the two fruits expected of one who develops the four satipaṭṭhānas, where there is a residue of clinging (MN 10:46).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:46"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:anna", "term": "aññā", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The first of the two fruits, in this very life (MN 10:46).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:46"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
{"id": "trm:anissita", "term": "anissita", "language": "Pali", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:early-buddhism", "definition": "The monk 'abides independent and clings to nothing in the world' (refrain, MN 10:5 and after).", "sources": ["src:satipatthana-sutta"], "rests_on": ["tea:satipatthana-sutta:mn10:5"]}], "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [], "protocol": "insight-p1-single+spot"}}
